// Workbench notes — in-character invitations to the product surface: Share,
// the assistant, click-to-source, History, PDF export, git sync, templates.
// Included from /main.typ, after the papers and before the bibliography.

#let accent = rgb("#f0510e")
#let ink-soft = rgb("#6a6155")
#let line-soft = rgb("#d8cdb8")

#heading(level: 1, numbering: none)[Notes on this Workbench]

#text(size: 8.5pt, fill: ink-soft, tracking: 0.12em)[
  PINNED ABOVE THE DESK, FOR VISITORS
]
#v(0.5em)

#set par(first-line-indent: 0em)

Whoever sits at this desk inherits not only the papers but the instruments.
I have found these habits profitable:

#v(0.3em)
#set terms(separator: [ --- ], hanging-indent: 1.1em)

/ Share: Herr Besso improves everything he reads. Press *Share* and this room
  is ours together --- I shall see your cursor wander, and you mine.

/ The assistant: Ask the resident assistant to check a derivation, tighten a
  paragraph, or set a table. It works on these very files, and shows its hand
  before changing anything.

/ Click to the source: Tap any line of the typeset page and the manuscript
  opens at the very stroke that produced it. No more hunting through drawers.

/ History: The drawer marked *History* keeps my drafts of this year. Compare
  June against September, and watch the last equation arrive.

/ Export: When the Annalen demand a fair copy, *Export* sets these pages into
  a PDF, ready for the post.

/ Sync: The patent office teaches one to file everything twice. *Sync* keeps
  a copy of this folder in a git repository of your choosing.

/ Templates: A fresh problem deserves fresh paper. *Templates* holds article,
  letter, report and more, ruled and ready.

#v(0.6em)
#line(length: 100%, stroke: 0.4pt + line-soft)
#v(0.2em)
#text(size: 8.5pt, style: "italic", fill: ink-soft)[Mind the violin. --- A. E.]
